    <div class="container">
      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Complementary Exercises for Dead Hang Training</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Dead hangs train support grip. These six exercises add moving support grip, pinch grip, pulling strength and forearm isolation.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div><div class="container">
      <h2>Why Add Complementary Exercises</h2>
      <p>Dead hangs train one grip type: support grip. Your fingers wrap the bar and resist opening under bodyweight load. This matters for hanging, deadlifts and carries. It is not the only grip capacity your hands use.</p>
      <p>Your hands produce force in three main patterns. Support grip holds a bar or handle against gravity. Crush grip squeezes an object between the fingers and palm. Pinch grip presses the thumb against the fingers. Dead hangs train the first pattern. Plate pinches add the third. A hand gripper from the <a href="/grip-attachments/">grip attachments guide</a> adds the second.</p>
      <p>Pulling strength also matters. Dead hangs build the static foundation. Your shoulders, back and biceps need dynamic pulling work such as pull-ups, chin-ups and rows.</p>
      <p>Adding a block of complementary exercises to your dead hang workout fills these gaps. The exercises below need minimal equipment and can improve grip strength over a few weeks. The sets, reps and loads on this page are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

      <h2>Farmer's Walks</h2>
      <p>Pick up a heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in each hand. Stand tall with your chest up and shoulders down. Walk at a controlled pace for 30 to 60 seconds. Set the weights down and rest 60 to 90 seconds. Perform 3 sets.</p>
      <p>Farmer's walks train support grip with a moving load, plus core stability and carrying capacity. Your fingers hold the handles against gravity while the weights shift during walking.</p>
      <p>A common starting load is 50 to 60% of your bodyweight split between both hands. An 80 kg person carries about 20 to 24 kg per hand. Increase the load gradually once you complete three 45-second walks with a controlled grip.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Walk on a flat surface with good traction. Avoid turns that twist your wrists.</li>
        <li>Keep your shoulders level. Do not lean to either side.</li>
        <li>Hold the handles firmly throughout the walk and set the weights down before your grip gives out.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Plate Pinches</h2>
      <p>Place two weight plates smooth-side-out and pinch them together between your thumb and fingers. Hold for 15 to 30 seconds per hand. Rest 45 seconds between hands. Perform 3 sets per hand.</p>
      <p>Plate pinches train the thumb and pinch grip. Dead hangs do not train these directly. Your thumb opposes your fingers to make a clamping force. This grip pattern is used for grabbing odd-shaped objects, climbing holds and grappling.</p>
      <p>Start with two 5 kg plates (about 11 lb) per hand. Add a small plate or use a thicker pair as you improve. The smooth metal surface makes pinching harder than the weight alone suggests.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep your arm at your side during the hold. Do not raise the plates or swing them.</li>
        <li>Chalk dries the skin and increases friction. Smooth plates and sweaty hands make this exercise frustrating without chalk.</li>
        <li>Alternate hands without rest if you want more training density. Your left hand rests while your right hand works.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Pull-Ups and Chin-Ups</h2>
      <p>Pull-ups and chin-ups are the natural next step from dead hangs. You already hold the bar at the bottom position. Now pull your chin above it. This turns your static grip and shoulder stability into dynamic pulling strength.</p>
      <p>Grip the bar overhand (pull-up) or underhand (chin-up) at shoulder width. Pull your body up until your chin clears the bar. Lower yourself under control back to a full dead hang. Repeat.</p>
      <p>Start with negative pull-ups if you cannot do a full pull-up yet. Jump to the top position and lower yourself as slowly as you can over 5 to 8 seconds. Perform 3 sets of 3 to 5 negatives. Progress varies between people.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Pull-ups (overhand) and chin-ups (underhand) use the arm muscles slightly differently. Train both variations.</li>
        <li>Start each rep from a dead hang position. Half reps that skip the bottom position cut short the grip and shoulder work.</li>
        <li>Add 2 to 3 sets of pull-ups after your dead hang sets on the days you train hangs. Build reps before adding weight.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Inverted Rows</h2>
      <p>Set a barbell in a rack at waist height. Hang underneath it with your arms extended and your body straight from head to heels. Pull your chest to the bar. Lower yourself back to full arm extension. Perform 3 sets of 8 to 12 reps.</p>
      <p>Inverted rows build horizontal pulling strength. Dead hangs and pull-ups train vertical pulling. Your mid-back muscles (rhomboids, mid-traps, rear deltoids) work in horizontal pulls. This balances the upper back work.</p>
      <p>Inverted rows are easier than pull-ups because you support less bodyweight. Adjust difficulty by changing your body angle. A more vertical torso reduces load and a more horizontal torso increases it.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep your body rigid from ankles to shoulders. No sagging at the hips. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core as if performing a plank.</li>
        <li>Pull your elbows back and not out. Aim to touch the bar at your lower chest and not your neck.</li>
        <li>Use an overhand grip for more forearm work or an underhand grip for more biceps work. Match the grip to your dead hang training goals.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Wrist Curls and Reverse Curls</h2>
      <p>Wrist curls train the forearm flexors that close your hand and hold the bar in a dead hang. Reverse curls train the forearm extensors that open the hand and balance the flexors.</p>

      <h3>Wrist Curls</h3>
      <p>Sit on a bench with your forearms resting on your thighs. Let your wrists hang over the edge with palms facing up. Curl a light barbell or dumbbells through full wrist flexion. Perform 2 to 3 sets of 12 to 15 reps with a 2-second pause at the top.</p>

      <h3>Reverse Wrist Curls</h3>
      <p>Use the same position with palms facing down. Extend the wrist upward against the weight. Use a lighter load than for wrist curls because the wrist extensors are weaker. Perform 2 to 3 sets of 12 to 15 reps.</p>
      <p>Dead hangs, farmer's walks and plate pinches all work the flexors. Some people add extensor work to balance flexor-heavy grip training; evidence that it prevents tennis elbow is limited.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Use slow and controlled reps. Momentum takes the work off the forearm.</li>
        <li>Keep the weight light. Leave the heavy loads for dead hangs and farmer's walks.</li>
        <li>Perform wrist curls at the end of your training session. Tiring the forearms before dead hangs shortens your hang time.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Scapular Pull-Ups</h2>
      <p>Scapular pull-ups bridge the gap between dead hangs and full pull-ups. You pull your shoulder blades down and together without bending your elbows. Your body rises a short distance. This practices the shoulder blade movement that starts a pull-up.</p>
      <p>Grip the bar overhand at shoulder width. Start in a passive dead hang with relaxed shoulders. Pull your shoulder blades down toward your back pockets. Hold the top position for 2 to 3 seconds. Return to the passive hang. Perform 3 sets of 8 to 10 reps.</p>
      <p>The lower trapezius, serratus anterior and lats help hold the shoulder blades down at the bottom of a pull-up. Scapular pull-ups train that range.</p>

      <h3>Programming Notes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep your arms completely straight throughout the movement. Any elbow bend means you are performing a partial pull-up instead.</li>
        <li>Focus on pulling your shoulder blades toward your back pockets. This cue gives the correct shoulder blade depression.</li>
        <li>Perform scapular pull-ups right after your dead hang sets. Your grip is warm and your shoulders are open.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sample Full Grip Workout</h2>
      <p>Combine dead hangs with complementary exercises in a single 30-minute session. This workout covers support grip, moving support grip, pinch grip, pulling strength and forearm isolation.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Exercise</th>
            <th scope="col">Sets x Reps/Duration</th>
            <th scope="col">Rest</th>
            <th scope="col">Grip Type</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Dead Hangs (controlled hold)</td>
            <td>4 x controlled hold</td>
            <td>90 seconds</td>
            <td>Support</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Scapular Pull-Ups</td>
            <td>3 x 8 reps</td>
            <td>60 seconds</td>
            <td>Support + pulling</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Farmer's Walks</td>
            <td>3 x 30 to 45 seconds</td>
            <td>60 seconds</td>
            <td>Support (moving)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Plate Pinches</td>
            <td>3 x 15 to 20 seconds/hand</td>
            <td>45 seconds</td>
            <td>Pinch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Wrist Curls</td>
            <td>2 x 15 reps</td>
            <td>45 seconds</td>
            <td>Isolation (flexors)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reverse Wrist Curls</td>
            <td>2 x 15 reps</td>
            <td>45 seconds</td>
            <td>Isolation (extensors)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Progress each exercise independently using the notes in its section above.</p>
      <p>Replace scapular pull-ups with full pull-ups or chin-ups once you can perform 3 clean reps. Add inverted rows on alternate training days for horizontal pulling balance. The <a href="/training-programs/">training programs</a> put these exercises into weekly plans.</p>

